you could call it the great divide when  it comes to the future of work ipsos  polling done exclusively for global news  suggests people who have worked remotely  during the pandemic are split on the  return to the office but united in  embracing flexible schedules many  canadians want the changes that the  pandemic brought to the workplace to be  permanent and for things to not go back  the way they were  before that fateful day in march 2020  when everything shut down in fact one in  three say they'd look for another job if  their employer forced them to work  exclusively at the office and yet 42  percent say they're happiest at their  place of work rather than working from  home the divide is pronounced among  younger workers nearly half of people  between the ages of 18 and 34 prefer to  be in the office rather than work  remotely now that may reflect a desire  to learn and connect with colleagues  from those starting out in their careers  but a little more than half of young  workers say they want to work from home  working remotely or in the workplace is  very much linked to the housing crisis  that we're seeing right now young people  are are struggling to save up for down  payments the average savings for remote  workers in 2021 was about two hundred  dollars a week or more than eleven  hundred dollars a year according to  cisco canada that's money saved on  commuting lunches out child care pet  care and wardrobe with a rapidly rising  cost of everything those numbers are  only going up attitudes on where and how  to work have shifted with 44 percent  saying their workplace now offers  flexible arrangements that didn't exist  pre-pandemic hybrid schedules are most  common in ontario where more than half  say their workplace has adopted a  flexible model followed by atlantic  canada and bc at 43 percent and quebec  38  this is one lasting change lasting  impact of the pandemic many appreciate  the new flexible working environment  want to stay that way and are willing to  change jobs to find it anne gaviola  global news toronto
 

